1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:01,969
Two in the morning.

2
00:00:01,969 --> 00:00:03,716
A NICU, somewhere in India.

3
00:00:03,916 --> 00:00:05,450
A baby girl, nine days old.

4
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:06,676
Born a little early.

5
00:00:06,676 --> 00:00:09,150
Fighting an infection in her blood.

6
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:10,966
The culture report comes back.

7
00:00:10,966 --> 00:00:12,962
Resistant. Resistant. Resistant.

8
00:00:13,162 --> 00:00:15,955
Outside the glass, her mother has been standing for

9
00:00:15,955 --> 00:00:16,538
hours.

10
00:00:16,738 --> 00:00:18,789
She asks just one question.

11
00:00:18,789 --> 00:00:20,903
Doctor, koi aur dawai hai?

12
00:00:21,653 --> 00:00:23,665
Is there any other medicine?

13
00:00:25,615 --> 00:00:27,809
This story does not begin in the NICU.

14
00:00:27,809 --> 00:00:29,741
It begins in rooms like ours.

15
00:00:29,941 --> 00:00:32,676
A Monday OPD. Forty patients still waiting.

16
00:00:32,676 --> 00:00:34,005
Fever for three days,

17
00:00:34,005 --> 00:00:34,958
runny nose.

18
00:00:35,158 --> 00:00:38,163
Doctor sahab, koi strong antibiotic de dijiye.

19
00:00:38,163 --> 00:00:39,757
Kal office jaana hai.

20
00:00:39,957 --> 00:00:41,104
We know it is viral.

21
00:00:41,104 --> 00:00:43,551
We are tired. And saying no takes time we

22
00:00:43,551 --> 00:00:44,489
do not have.

23
00:00:44,689 --> 00:00:47,377
Azithromycin. Three days. Next patient.

24
00:00:47,577 --> 00:00:49,259
Ten seconds. One signature.

25
00:00:49,259 --> 00:00:50,886
It felt like kindness.

26
00:00:51,636 --> 00:00:55,326
Across India, that signature is written again and again.

27
00:00:55,526 --> 00:00:58,051
In a national survey of twenty hospitals,

28
00:00:58,051 --> 00:01:01,494
seven in ten admitted patients were on antibiotics.

29
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:02,919
More than half of them,

30
00:01:02,919 --> 00:01:03,849
just in case.

31
00:01:04,049 --> 00:01:06,634
Every dose that was not needed is a lesson.

32
00:01:06,634 --> 00:01:09,088
And bacteria never forget a lesson.

33
00:01:09,288 --> 00:01:12,874
In 2017, imipenem, one of our last-line drugs,

34
00:01:12,874 --> 00:01:16,405
still worked on about six in ten Klebsiella samples.

35
00:01:16,605 --> 00:01:19,417
By 2023, barely one in three.

36
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:22,524
The man with the cold got better in a

37
00:01:22,524 --> 00:01:24,336
week. He always would have.

38
00:01:24,536 --> 00:01:26,088
But the bacteria stayed.

39
00:01:26,088 --> 00:01:27,407
In families. In wards.

40
00:01:27,407 --> 00:01:28,433
In our water.

41
00:01:28,633 --> 00:01:31,126
And they found the ones who could not fight

42
00:01:31,126 --> 00:01:31,661
back.

43
00:01:31,861 --> 00:01:34,123
A newborn. A mother after her C-section.

44
00:01:34,123 --> 00:01:36,198
A grandfather on a ventilator.

45
00:01:36,398 --> 00:01:38,659
They never asked for that antibiotic.

46
00:01:38,659 --> 00:01:41,024
They inherited what it left behind.

47
00:01:42,174 --> 00:01:43,060
Back in the NICU,

48
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:45,629
her mother is still waiting for an answer.

49
00:01:45,829 --> 00:01:48,849
Every prescription we write today is our answer to

50
00:01:48,849 --> 00:01:49,315
her.

51
00:01:50,465 --> 00:01:53,249
Antimicrobial stewardship is not a committee,

52
00:01:53,249 --> 00:01:55,284
or a circular. It is that answer.

53
00:01:55,484 --> 00:01:57,875
Pause, and ask: is this really bacterial?

54
00:01:58,075 --> 00:02:00,633
Send the culture before the first dose.

55
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:02,001
When the report comes,

56
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:03,939
narrow the drug, and stop on time.

57
00:02:04,139 --> 00:02:05,094
For surgery, one dose.

58
00:02:05,094 --> 00:02:05,962
Not five days.

59
00:02:06,162 --> 00:02:07,572
And teach every patient:

60
00:02:07,572 --> 00:02:09,898
the red line on the strip means only with

61
00:02:09,898 --> 00:02:11,064
a prescription.

62
00:02:12,014 --> 00:02:13,817
AMR Awareness Week has ended.

63
00:02:13,817 --> 00:02:16,395
The bacteria did not take the week off.

64
00:02:16,595 --> 00:02:19,187
This is not one more guideline to forward on

65
00:02:19,187 --> 00:02:20,661
WhatsApp and forget.

66
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:22,461
It is ten seconds of courage.

67
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:23,505
To explain. To wait.

68
00:02:23,505 --> 00:02:24,242
To say no.

69
00:02:24,442 --> 00:02:25,606
So that one day,

70
00:02:25,606 --> 00:02:27,062
when a mother asks,

71
00:02:27,062 --> 00:02:28,670
Koi aur dawai hai?

72
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,292
we can still say:

73
00:02:31,292 --> 00:02:32,443
Haan. Hai.
